# Realtime OpenWorm Simulation Plan

This task file is the plan for a new realtime simulator that uses the existing OpenWorm connectome data and simulator rules, but is explicitly optimized for interactive/realtime execution on an NVIDIA RTX 4090-class GPU or larger.

This is a separate target from `.tasks/nvidia_accelerate` and `.tasks/apple_accelerate`. Those plans preserve behavior identity with the current 5 us timestep c302/Sibernetic stack. This plan targets realtime behavior with validation against that stack, not byte-identical reproduction of every particle-level intermediate state.

Official hardware references checked for this plan:

- NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 specs: https://www.nvidia.com/en-gb/geforce/graphics-cards/40-series/rtx-4090/
- NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090 specs: https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/50-series/rtx-5090/
- NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell specs: https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/products/workstations/professional-desktop-gpus/rtx-pro-6000/
- NVIDIA H200 specs: https://www.nvidia.com/en-gb/data-center/h200/

## Objective

Create a realtime simulator that can run `150 ms` of worm simulation in `150 ms` or less on a single RTX 4090 when possible, with a larger NVIDIA GPU tier allowed if the validated model requires it.

The simulator must use:

- The existing connectome source data.
- The existing c302 FW/C2 model rules as the neural/model-construction reference.
- The existing 96 muscle activation convention.
- The existing Sibernetic body/muscle/environment rules as the physical reference.
- The existing output concepts: muscle activity, worm motion, visualization, video, and streamable frames.

The simulator does not need to be byte-identical to Sibernetic/c302 at the particle-buffer level. It must instead meet quantified behavior-matching criteria against the exact/reference simulator.

## Definitions

### Exact Simulator

The exact simulator is the legacy-compatible c302/Sibernetic implementation or the future accelerated exact implementation from `.tasks/nvidia_accelerate`. It preserves the 5 us timestep, c302-generated neural model, Sibernetic particle/body state, and legacy outputs.

Use exact mode for:

- Ground-truth fixture generation.
- Scientific compatibility checks.
- Regression tests for parser/model changes.
- Training data generation for realtime mode.

### Realtime Simulator

The realtime simulator is a validated approximation that consumes the same connectome/model rules and produces comparable behavior-level outputs within explicit tolerances.

Use realtime mode for:

- Interactive exploration.
- Realtime visualization.
- Parameter sweeps where approximate behavior is acceptable.
- Demonstrations and streamed output.
- Later interactive scientific workflows after validation is strong enough.

### Behavioral Identity

Behavioral identity means the output is indistinguishable for the metrics that matter for the intended workflow, not that every particle state matches.

Required behavior classes:

- Neural/muscle timing.
- Dorsal/ventral and left/right phase.
- Centerline shape.
- Body curvature wave propagation.
- Locomotion speed and direction.
- Response to stimulation and perturbation.
- Visual muscle activation and body motion.

### Hard Realtime

For this plan, hard realtime means:

- `150 ms` simulated time completes in `<=150 ms` wall time after warmup.
- The p95 latency of repeated `150 ms` windows is `<=150 ms`.
- The p99 latency target is tracked and must be `<=200 ms` before release.

### Preferred Realtime

Preferred realtime means:

- `150 ms` simulated time completes in `<=25 ms` wall time headless after warmup.
- The renderer can use the remaining budget to sustain 60 FPS or better.

## Realtime Reality Check

The legacy default timestep is `dt=0.005 ms`.

For a `150 ms` simulation:

```text
150 ms / 0.005 ms = 30,000 simulation steps
150 ms wall time / 30,000 steps = 5 microseconds per full step
```

A full legacy step includes neural update, particle hash/sort, grid index, neighbor search, density, forces, elastic/muscle coupling, three PCISPH correction loops, integration, membrane interactions, logging decisions, and optional rendering. A literal byte-identical realtime implementation would need every complete step to finish in about `5 us`, which is not a realistic engineering target even on high-end GPUs.

Therefore this plan uses a realtime validated-model strategy:

- Preserve the biological/connectome and simulator-rule inputs.
- Use the exact legacy/accelerated simulator as the oracle.
- Build a reduced or surrogate simulator that matches behavior-level outputs.
- Keep exact mode and realtime mode separate in CLI, docs, tests, and output manifests.

## Design Principles

- Start with mechanistic structure and add learning only where measurements show systematic error.
- Preserve connectome and c302 model-construction rules exactly, even if body dynamics are reduced.
- Preserve the 96-channel muscle activation API so exact, fast, and realtime backends can share tooling.
- Prefer stable low-dimensional body state over high-dimensional particle state for realtime.
- Make every approximation explicit in the run manifest.
- Validate in rollout, not only one-step prediction.
- Optimize only after the validation harness exists.
- Keep training, validation, and hidden test runs separated from the beginning.
- Do not let rendering frame rate hide simulation error.
- Do not use larger hardware before profiling proves the model is compute-bound or memory-bound.

