## Recommended Realtime Model

Use a hybrid mechanistic plus learned-residual architecture.

### Neural Runtime

Start with a compiled deterministic neural runtime derived from the c302-generated FW/C2 model.

Options, in order:

1. Generate C++/CUDA code from the exact generated c302/LEMS model.
2. Convert the generated model to a compact graph of ODE/state update equations.
3. Run the 39-neuron plus muscle-state model in a CUDA kernel or small CUDA Graph.
4. Add tensor-core acceleration only if the math is reformulated and validation still passes.

Avoid Python/NEURON in realtime mode. Keep it only for reference data generation.

Realtime neural acceptance:

- Produces 96 muscle activations.
- Matches NEURON/c302 reference within defined tolerances over short and long windows.
- Preserves phase, amplitude, calcium saturation, and response to current pulses.
- Runs in less than `0.5 ms` per `150 ms` simulated window after warmup.

### Body Runtime

Do not attempt full Sibernetic SPH in realtime mode unless benchmarks prove it can hit the budget.

Preferred model:

- A reduced centerline/body model with approximately 48-128 longitudinal segments.
- Segment state includes position, orientation, curvature, velocity, and local environment contact state.
- Muscle state maps the 96 activation channels onto dorsal/ventral left/right bending forces.
- Use a Cosserat-rod, mass-spring chain, or finite-segment elastic-body model.
- Add simple calibrated contact/friction/drag models for crawling.
- Add a learned residual model to correct systematic differences from Sibernetic.

Why this is the right starting point:

- The visual/behavior outputs are centerline and muscle activity, not every SPH particle.
- The current Sibernetic config has tens of thousands of particles and expensive neighbor dynamics.
- Realtime requires reducing state dimensionality while preserving behavior metrics.

Realtime body acceptance:

- Advances `150 ms` simulated time in less than `5 ms` wall time on RTX 4090 after warmup.
- Preserves bend wavelength, bend amplitude, propagation speed, locomotion speed, and phase relative to muscle activation.
- Produces a legacy-compatible `worm_motion_log` export.

### Learned Residual Or Surrogate

Use learning to correct the reduced model, not to replace all structure at first.

Recommended residual inputs:

- Current centerline state.
- Segment velocities.
- 96 muscle activations.
- Recent activation history.
- Contact/environment state.
- Configuration identifier.
- Optional latent state from prior frames.

Recommended residual outputs:

- Per-segment force correction.
- Per-segment curvature correction.
- Next-window centerline correction.
- Confidence/uncertainty estimate.

Candidate model families:

- Small temporal convolution network over the body axis.
- Graph neural network over segment adjacency and muscle-channel attachments.
- State-space model with CUDA/TensorRT inference.
- Tiny transformer only if sequence context is clearly needed and latency fits.

Keep model size small enough that inference is not the bottleneck. Prefer a model that runs in under `1 ms` per `150 ms` simulated window on RTX 4090.

## Model Candidate Matrix

Evaluate these body model candidates in order. Do not commit to a learned-only model before the mechanistic baselines are measured.

### Candidate A - 2D Centerline Rod

Description:

- 2D body centerline with curvature, local tangent, and segment velocity.
- Muscle channels drive dorsal/ventral bending.
- Contact/friction model handles crawling.

Pros:

- Fastest.
- Easiest to fit and debug.
- Good for initial realtime proof.

Cons:

- Cannot capture 3D roll/twist or full body-environment interactions.
- May miss Sibernetic artifacts from particle/fluid coupling.

Gate:

- Use as first milestone if it matches bend frequency, phase, and centerline error on default crawling.

### Candidate B - 3D Cosserat Rod

Description:

- 3D segment pose and orientation model with bending/twist stiffness.
- Muscle groups produce torques around the body.
- Contact model handles ground interaction.

Pros:

- Strong physical structure.
- Can represent richer body motion than 2D.
- Still much smaller than SPH particles.

Cons:

- More parameters to fit.
- Harder contact model.

Gate:

- Use if 2D centerline cannot match held-out trajectories or if visual/streaming requirements need 3D posture.

### Candidate C - Mass-Spring Segment Chain

Description:

- A chain or tube mesh with springs, dampers, and muscle actuation.
- Similar conceptual lineage to Sibernetic elastic connections, but far fewer nodes.

Pros:

- Easier to map from legacy muscle forces.
- Straightforward GPU implementation.

Cons:

- Can become unstable without small timesteps.
- May need many substeps, threatening realtime.

Gate:

- Use if Cosserat model is too hard to fit or if spring semantics map better to Sibernetic fixtures.

### Candidate D - Learned Dynamics Model

Description:

- Model predicts next reduced state from current state and muscle history.
- Could be graph, temporal convolution, state-space model, or compact transformer.

Pros:

- Potentially fastest and most accurate inside training distribution.
- Can absorb complex Sibernetic effects.

Cons:

- Weak extrapolation.
- Harder to trust scientifically.
- Requires large high-quality reference dataset.

Gate:

- Use only as residual first. Promote to standalone only if it passes hidden perturbation tests and uncertainty checks.

### Candidate E - Multi-Rate Hybrid

Description:

- Neural state updates at a higher rate.
- Body state updates at a lower rate.
- Residual correction runs per render frame or per 150 ms window.

Pros:

- Best latency/accuracy tradeoff if neural dynamics need finer time resolution than body dynamics.

Cons:

- Adds phase-alignment complexity.

Gate:

- Use if compiled neural runtime is cheap but body substeps dominate.

## Numerical And Precision Plan

Realtime mode can use lower precision only after validation proves it does not change behavior metrics beyond tolerance.

Initial precision:

- `float32` for neural state.
- `float32` for body state.
- `float32` for muscle activations.
- `float32` for residual model inference.

Allowed after validation:

- TF32 for learned residual matrix operations.
- FP16/BF16 for residual inference if rollout metrics pass.
- Mixed precision for rendering-only data.

Not allowed without explicit task:

- FP16 neural dynamics if phase or activation saturation changes.
- Quantized body dynamics if it destabilizes rollout.
- Fast-math changes without metric comparison.

Each precision mode must have:

- a manifest field.
- a benchmark result.
- a validation scorecard.
- a release note if user-visible.

## Runtime Architecture

### Process Layout

- `ow-realtime run`
  - Runs realtime simulation.
- `ow-realtime validate`
  - Compares realtime output to exact/reference fixtures.
- `ow-realtime train`
  - Trains residual/surrogate models from reference data.
- `ow-realtime render`
  - Renders outputs offline.
- `ow-realtime stream`
  - Streams live rendered frames.

### Core Runtime Components

- `connectome/`
  - Exact c302-compatible connectome parser and model builder.
- `neural/`
  - Compiled FW/C2 neural runtime.
  - Legacy NEURON reference adapter.
- `body/`
  - Reduced mechanistic body model.
  - Residual/surrogate model.
  - Export adapter to legacy motion logs.
- `cuda/`
  - CUDA kernels.
  - CUDA Graphs for fixed update sequences.
  - Optional TensorRT/CUDA inference path.
- `render/`
  - Real-time renderer.
  - Headless renderer.
  - NVENC video/stream encoding.
- `validation/`
  - Golden fixture comparison.
  - Behavior metrics.
  - Latency benchmarks.

### Dataflow

1. Load connectome CSV and c302 FW/C2 rules.
2. Build neural model IR.
3. Load realtime body model parameters.
4. Load optional learned residual weights.
5. For each realtime window:
   - advance neural state and produce 96 muscle activations.
   - map activations to body segments.
   - advance reduced body dynamics.
   - apply residual correction.
   - export centerline/motion state.
   - render or stream the latest frame.
6. Periodically emit legacy-compatible outputs for validation and tooling.

## Latency Budget

Hard product target:

- `150 ms` simulated time in `<=150 ms` wall time on RTX 4090.

Engineering target:

- `150 ms` simulated time in `<=25 ms` wall time on RTX 4090, leaving room for UI, streaming, parameter changes, and slower machines.

Suggested per-window budget:

- Neural runtime: `<=0.5 ms`.
- Body reduced dynamics: `<=5 ms`.
- Residual/surrogate inference: `<=1 ms`.
- State export and metrics: `<=1 ms`.
- Rendering: `<=4 ms` for 60 FPS, optional if headless.
- Encoding/streaming: `<=8 ms` with NVENC, optional if headless.
- Scheduler/overhead: `<=5 ms`.

If the design cannot hit `<=25 ms` per 150 ms simulated window on RTX 4090, profile before moving to larger hardware. Larger hardware should not be used to compensate for retaining a particle-level exact model in realtime mode.

## Accuracy Budget

Initial tolerances should be conservative placeholders. They must be revised after the first exact/reference dataset is generated and baseline variability is measured.

Suggested first-pass public validation thresholds:

- Muscle activation normalized RMSE: `<=0.08` over 150 ms windows.
- Muscle activation phase error: `<=10 degrees` for dominant oscillatory components.
- Centerline point RMSE: `<=5 percent` of body length over 150 ms windows.
- Curvature RMSE: `<=10 percent` of reference curvature dynamic range.
- Bend amplitude error: `<=10 percent`.
- Bend frequency error: `<=5 percent`.
- Bend wavelength error: `<=10 percent`.
- Forward locomotion speed error: `<=10 percent`.
- Heading error: `<=10 degrees` over 150 ms windows.
- Long rollout drift: no unphysical self-intersection or exploding curvature over 10 s.

Stricter internal targets:

- Centerline point RMSE: `<=2 percent` of body length.
- Locomotion speed error: `<=5 percent`.
- Bend frequency error: `<=2 percent`.
- Stable rollout for `60 s` with no manual reset.

The validation harness must report the full metric distribution, not only pass/fail.

## Reference Dataset Design

The realtime model will only be as good as the exact/reference coverage. Build the dataset deliberately.

### Dataset Splits

- `train`
  - Used for fitting body parameters and residual models.
- `validation`
  - Used for model selection and hyperparameter tuning.
- `public_test`
  - Used in CI and release scorecards.
- `hidden_test`
  - Used only before release to catch overfitting.

### Required Scenarios

- Default FW/C2 crawling, `worm_crawl_half_resolution`.
- No-c302 sine muscle drive.
- Worm-alone half-resolution.
- Short default runs of `150 ms`.
- Medium runs of `1 s`.
- Long stability runs of `10 s`, `30 s`, and eventually `60 s`.

### Perturbation Scenarios

- Initial posture perturbations:
  - straight.
  - shallow bend.
  - strong bend.
  - shifted center of mass.
- Neural perturbations:
  - AVB stimulation changes.
  - DB/VB current amplitude changes.
  - selected neuron silencing.
  - selected connection scaling.
- Muscle perturbations:
  - single muscle group gain changes.
  - dorsal-only drive.
  - ventral-only drive.
  - left/right imbalance.
- Environment perturbations:
  - friction coefficient changes.
  - drag coefficient changes.
  - agar/carpet parameter changes.
  - boundary/contact changes.
- Numerical perturbations:
  - timestep variations in exact/fast reference where supported.
  - logstep variations.

### Dataset Artifacts

For each reference run, store:

- `run_manifest.json`.
- connectome/model hashes.
- c302 generated artifact hashes.
- 96-channel muscle activity.
- centerline and curvature time series.
- reduced state extraction from particle data.
- final WCON.
- selected rendered frames.
- optional raw particle snapshots at sparse intervals.
- validation summary for the exact run itself.

### Dataset Size Targets

Milestone dataset:

- 20-50 reference runs.
- Enough for model architecture decisions.

Prototype dataset:

- 200-500 reference runs.
- Enough to fit first residual model.

Release dataset:

- 1,000+ reference runs if learned residuals are product-facing.
- Hidden test coverage across perturbations.

If exact reference generation is too slow, prioritize diversity over duration and use the accelerated exact backend as soon as it exists.

## Calibration Workflow

The realtime model should be calibrated in layers.

### Layer 1 - Muscle Mapping

- [ ] Map 96 muscle channels onto reduced body segments.
- [ ] Preserve dorsal/ventral and left/right grouping.
- [ ] Fit per-muscle gain and delay only if reference data proves they are needed.
- [ ] Keep default gains interpretable and documented.

### Layer 2 - Passive Body Parameters

- [ ] Fit stiffness.
- [ ] Fit damping.
- [ ] Fit segment mass or effective inertia.
- [ ] Fit bending/twist resistance.
- [ ] Validate passive relaxation from perturbed initial postures.

### Layer 3 - Contact And Environment

- [ ] Fit crawling friction.
- [ ] Fit normal/contact response.
- [ ] Fit fluid/drag approximation if swimming is included later.
- [ ] Validate locomotion speed and heading under varied environment parameters.

### Layer 4 - Active Muscle Dynamics

- [ ] Fit muscle force scale.
- [ ] Fit activation-to-force nonlinearity.
- [ ] Fit activation delay if necessary.
- [ ] Fit saturation behavior.
- [ ] Validate phase and amplitude of bending waves.

### Layer 5 - Residual Model

- [ ] Train residual only on errors left after mechanistic calibration.
- [ ] Constrain residual magnitude.
- [ ] Penalize energy injection and unstable curvature.
- [ ] Validate long-horizon rollout.
- [ ] Add uncertainty estimation.

### Layer 6 - Runtime Compression

- [ ] Remove unused model inputs.
- [ ] Fuse kernels.
- [ ] Capture CUDA Graphs.
- [ ] Quantize residual model only if validation passes.
- [ ] Freeze release artifacts and hashes.

